1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,567
A doctor checks your temperature.

2
00:00:02,567 --> 00:00:04,242
A shopkeeper weighs rice.

3
00:00:04,242 --> 00:00:05,676
You measure a table.

4
00:00:06,076 --> 00:00:08,017
Three very different jobs.

5
00:00:08,017 --> 00:00:10,869
But all three use the same basic idea.

6
00:00:11,319 --> 00:00:13,491
Even Diya's recipe card needs it.

7
00:00:13,491 --> 00:00:15,151
It says: sugar, two.

8
00:00:15,501 --> 00:00:16,466
Two spoons?

9
00:00:16,466 --> 00:00:17,366
Two cups?

10
00:00:17,325 --> 00:00:18,447
Two kilograms?

11
00:00:18,447 --> 00:00:20,650
The number alone means nothing.

12
00:00:21,150 --> 00:00:23,948
By the end of this
video, you will see why.

13
00:00:23,948 --> 00:00:26,233
It is the first big idea in physics.

14
00:00:26,883 --> 00:00:28,961
So what is a physical quantity?

15
00:00:29,311 --> 00:00:32,038
It is anything in nature
that we can measure.

16
00:00:32,038 --> 00:00:34,640
Length, mass, time, temperature, speed.

17
00:00:34,990 --> 00:00:38,694
Kindness and beauty matter, but
no instrument can measure them.

18
00:00:38,694 --> 00:00:40,871
So they are not physical quantities.

19
00:00:41,471 --> 00:00:43,508
Every measurement has two parts.

20
00:00:43,508 --> 00:00:45,592
A number, called the magnitude.

21
00:00:45,592 --> 00:00:46,521
And a unit.

22
00:00:46,871 --> 00:00:51,842
A unit is a fixed amount we all agree
on, like one metre or one kilogram.

23
00:00:52,492 --> 00:00:55,286
Let's measure one pencil
on our measuring bench.

24
00:00:55,636 --> 00:00:58,487
With a centimetre scale, it reads fifteen.

25
00:00:58,487 --> 00:00:59,847
Fifteen centimetres.

26
00:01:00,247 --> 00:01:01,667
Switch to millimetres.

27
00:01:01,667 --> 00:01:03,914
Now it reads one hundred and fifty.

28
00:01:04,314 --> 00:01:05,596
Switch to metres.

29
00:01:05,596 --> 00:01:08,079
Now it is only zero point one five.

30
00:01:08,479 --> 00:01:10,027
The pencil never changed.

31
00:01:10,027 --> 00:01:12,446
The number and the unit changed together.

32
00:01:12,946 --> 00:01:14,243
Look at the graph.

33
00:01:14,243 --> 00:01:17,257
Unit ten times bigger,
number ten times smaller.

34
00:01:17,907 --> 00:01:20,731
Let's write this as a
rule, one step at a time.

35
00:01:21,081 --> 00:01:22,043
Step one.

36
00:01:22,043 --> 00:01:26,217
A quantity Q equals its
number n, times its unit u.

37
00:01:26,217 --> 00:01:27,554
Q equals n u.

38
00:01:28,304 --> 00:01:29,204
Step two.

39
00:01:29,191 --> 00:01:33,193
The same quantity in two
different units does not change.

40
00:01:33,193 --> 00:01:35,952
So n one u one equals n two u two.

41
00:01:36,702 --> 00:01:37,615
Step three.

42
00:01:37,615 --> 00:01:41,456
n times u is fixed, so n is
inversely proportional to u.

43
00:01:41,456 --> 00:01:43,956
A bigger unit means a smaller number.

44
00:01:44,706 --> 00:01:45,733
Remember this.

45
00:01:45,733 --> 00:01:50,374
A physical quantity is always a number
with a unit, never a number alone.

46
00:01:51,074 --> 00:01:54,294
Now, physicists sort
quantities in two big ways.

47
00:01:54,644 --> 00:01:57,963
The first way: base quantities
and derived quantities.

48
00:01:58,313 --> 00:02:00,674
Base quantities are the building blocks.

49
00:02:00,674 --> 00:02:02,818
The SI system picks seven of them.

50
00:02:03,168 --> 00:02:07,653
Length, mass, time, electric current,
temperature, amount of substance,

51
00:02:07,653 --> 00:02:09,149
and luminous intensity.

52
00:02:09,549 --> 00:02:12,245
Derived quantities are built from these.

53
00:02:12,245 --> 00:02:13,195
Take speed.

54
00:02:13,545 --> 00:02:16,395
Speed equals distance divided by time.

55
00:02:16,795 --> 00:02:19,916
Distance is a length, so
it is a base quantity.

56
00:02:19,916 --> 00:02:21,864
Time is also a base quantity.

57
00:02:22,264 --> 00:02:25,003
So speed is made from two base quantities.

58
00:02:25,003 --> 00:02:26,997
That is why speed is derived.

59
00:02:27,397 --> 00:02:28,448
Side by side.

60
00:02:28,448 --> 00:02:29,598
Length is base.

61
00:02:29,598 --> 00:02:31,858
You read it straight off a ruler.

62
00:02:32,258 --> 00:02:33,413
Speed is derived.

63
00:02:33,413 --> 00:02:36,581
You need a length and a
time, and then you divide.

64
00:02:37,181 --> 00:02:38,221
Now you try.

65
00:02:38,221 --> 00:02:41,693
Is temperature a base quantity
or a derived quantity?

66
00:02:41,693 --> 00:02:43,612
Pause the video and decide.

67
00:02:46,812 --> 00:02:47,712
Base!

68
00:02:47,343 --> 00:02:49,440
Temperature is one of the seven.

69
00:02:49,440 --> 00:02:53,739
A thermometer reads it directly, and
it is not built from length, mass

70
00:02:53,739 --> 00:02:54,639
or time.

71
00:02:55,119 --> 00:02:57,438
The second way: scalars and vectors.

72
00:02:57,788 --> 00:02:59,830
A scalar has only a size.

73
00:02:59,830 --> 00:03:03,157
Five kilograms of rice
is just five kilograms.

74
00:03:03,157 --> 00:03:04,726
No direction needed.

75
00:03:05,076 --> 00:03:07,591
A vector has a size and a direction.

76
00:03:07,591 --> 00:03:10,534
Displacement, velocity
and force are vectors.

77
00:03:10,934 --> 00:03:13,432
Push a box with ten newtons to the right.

78
00:03:13,432 --> 00:03:14,470
It moves right.

79
00:03:14,820 --> 00:03:18,823
Push with the same ten newtons
to the left, and it moves left.

80
00:03:18,823 --> 00:03:20,706
Same size, different result.

81
00:03:20,706 --> 00:03:22,503
So force needs a direction.

82
00:03:23,153 --> 00:03:24,090
Your turn.

83
00:03:24,090 --> 00:03:26,587
Is velocity a scalar or a vector?

84
00:03:26,587 --> 00:03:27,964
Pause and decide.

85
00:03:31,164 --> 00:03:32,064
A vector!

86
00:03:32,012 --> 00:03:34,721
Velocity is speed in a given direction.

87
00:03:34,721 --> 00:03:38,980
Fifty kilometres per hour north
is not the same as fifty south.

88
00:03:39,630 --> 00:03:41,900
Let's sort six quantities on the board.

89
00:03:42,250 --> 00:03:44,569
Mass and time are base, and scalar.

90
00:03:44,919 --> 00:03:47,636
Speed and density are derived, and scalar.

91
00:03:47,986 --> 00:03:51,373
Velocity and force are
derived, and they are vectors.

92
00:03:51,723 --> 00:03:53,626
Notice one box stays empty.

93
00:03:53,626 --> 00:03:56,722
None of the seven base
quantities is a vector.

94
00:03:57,372 --> 00:03:59,107
Now, one worked example.

95
00:03:59,457 --> 00:04:02,312
A steel rod is two point five metres long.

96
00:04:02,312 --> 00:04:04,396
How long is it in centimetres?

97
00:04:04,896 --> 00:04:06,024
Use the rule.

98
00:04:06,024 --> 00:04:09,702
n two equals n one times
u one, divided by u two.

99
00:04:10,452 --> 00:04:12,477
Here n one is two point five.

100
00:04:12,477 --> 00:04:13,909
u one is one metre.

101
00:04:13,909 --> 00:04:15,581
u two is one centimetre.

102
00:04:16,331 --> 00:04:19,090
One metre is one hundred centimetres.

103
00:04:19,090 --> 00:04:22,567
So n two is two point
five times one hundred.

104
00:04:23,317 --> 00:04:25,267
That is two hundred and fifty.

105
00:04:25,267 --> 00:04:28,308
The rod is two hundred and
fifty centimetres long.

106
00:04:28,958 --> 00:04:30,424
Now, the exam trap.

107
00:04:30,774 --> 00:04:35,698
Many students say electric current is a
vector, because it flows in a direction.

108
00:04:36,048 --> 00:04:37,742
But current is a scalar.

109
00:04:37,742 --> 00:04:42,069
At a junction, two amperes and three
amperes always make five amperes,

110
00:04:42,069 --> 00:04:43,404
whatever the angle.

111
00:04:43,404 --> 00:04:45,399
Vectors do not add like that.

112
00:04:46,049 --> 00:04:47,411
Where is this asked?

113
00:04:47,411 --> 00:04:51,737
As quick one mark questions: spot the
base quantity, the derived one, or

114
00:04:51,737 --> 00:04:52,637
the vector.

115
00:04:53,179 --> 00:04:54,463
Exam level now.

116
00:04:54,463 --> 00:04:58,651
Which set has only derived
quantities that are also scalars?

117
00:04:58,651 --> 00:04:59,989
Pause and solve.

118
00:05:05,389 --> 00:05:06,736
The answer is A.

119
00:05:06,736 --> 00:05:08,603
Speed, density and work.

120
00:05:09,103 --> 00:05:11,490
B has mass and time, both base.

121
00:05:11,490 --> 00:05:13,291
C has force, a vector.

122
00:05:13,791 --> 00:05:16,575
D has current and temperature, both base.

123
00:05:16,575 --> 00:05:19,320
Only A is fully derived and fully scalar.

124
00:05:19,920 --> 00:05:21,380
Here is your recap.

125
00:05:21,380 --> 00:05:22,882
Screenshot this card.

126
00:05:26,082 --> 00:05:29,080
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

127
00:05:29,480 --> 00:05:30,380
One.

128
00:05:29,951 --> 00:05:32,934
Why is sugar, two, not
a complete measurement?

129
00:05:33,834 --> 00:05:34,734
Two.

130
00:05:34,348 --> 00:05:36,575
Why is speed a derived quantity?

131
00:05:37,475 --> 00:05:38,375
Three.

132
00:05:38,073 --> 00:05:41,454
Is electric current a
scalar or a vector, and why?

133
00:05:42,554 --> 00:05:46,964
Next time: if speed is built from
length and time, what is force built

134
00:05:46,964 --> 00:05:47,864
from?

135
00:05:47,502 --> 00:05:50,605
We find out in fundamental
and derived quantities.
